\pard\b\fs20 Fields of Glory, super undead\b0\par
\fs16\par
In a small valley lies the peaceful little town of Clonkolou, whose citizens work hard every day to increase their prosperity.\par
Their high-quality steel and their especially sulfurous flints brought the citizens an unexpected windfall of clunkers when, in spring, two great armies passed through their valley one after the other.\par
Then, at the first sunrise of the following autumn, the first cannonballs struck the bridge in the middle of the village.\par
After costly battles, the armies had returned to replenish their reserves, and they reached the slopes of the valley at the same time from two different sides.\par
After a first grueling skirmish in the streets of the town, the knights withdrew to their fortified positions to plan the final, decisive blow against the enemy.\par
The positions have been built up with the last of the resources, and the knights know they have only one attempt to storm the opposite slope and crush the enemy.\par
The coming winter makes a retreat impossible. Clonkolou lies in ruins, and citizens and knights alike know that hardly any of them will still have to worry about the hardship of the coming winter.\par
Each player has only four knights: whoever dies four times should count on being dead. Or not.\par
If you manage to kill three enemy knights, you receive a Heal group scroll.\par
For six kills your knight is upgraded to a king, or to a black knight if he is wearing armor; if you are controlling a skeleton, it becomes a skeleton king.\par
The first commandment in Clonkolou is: do not script with rejoins.\par
Somebody did it anyway, and now the dead live.\par
For every enemy knight killed, the killer gets a skeleton added to his crew.\par
Skeleton grades:\par
Every upgrade requires a grade 1 skeleton\par
-Grade 1: skeleton\par
-Grade 2: skeleton sorcerer\par
-Grade 3: skeleton knight\par
